#
# prima-materia - the Taulipang transmission of the Zauberarzt
#
# The independence-driven entrance of SPEC §14, and the testability-driven one
# at the same time. What this corpus supplies, and what made it worth opening
# rather than another Eurasian handbook, is the thing the holding is shortest
# of: named, mechanical conditions under which a capacity is destroyed, stated
# by the transmission in advance and tied to a named substance.
#
# Two of them, and both are permanent. A novice who drinks the beer brewed with
# the leaves of wolele-yeg has his whole throat burnt so that he can never sing
# again, which ends him as a Zauberarzt, because the office is carried out by
# singing. A finished Zauberarzt who eats piranha has the ladder to the sky cut
# so that he can never ascend again. Neither is a caution about danger in
# general; each names the substance, the mechanism and the capacity lost.
#
# Why a new tradition and not a place in an existing one. The holding carries no
# Carib-speaking people and nothing from the Guiana shield. traditions/jibaro-
# shuara.ttl, entered on 2026-10-02, is lowland Ecuador and Jivaroan, and its
# own file says that putting one people under a regional name would claim the
# region for them; the same reasoning forbids putting the Taulipang under the
# Jibaro name or under pmt:Amazonian, which stays registered and stays at zero.
#
# Order of the file: the two works; the tradition with the three decisions §14
# requires; the apprenticeship and what it claims about its own length; the sign
# announced in advance and the criterion of release; the two conditions of
# failure; the soul-journey and its two placements on the consciousness axes;
# the condition that aborts a cure; the jaguar; the two reports in which a
# practitioner checked his own result; and last the two things the spells say
# about who may use them and why the animals are invoked.
#

#
# The works
#

pmw:VomRoroimaZumOrinocoIII a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Koch-Gruenberg, Vom Roroima zum Orinoco, volume III: Ethnographie"@en, "Koch-Gruenberg, Vom Roroima zum Orinoco, Band III: Ethnographie"@de ;
    skos:note "The edition as the title page in the full text gives it: Vom Roroima zum Orinoco, Ergebnisse einer Reise in Nordbrasilien und Venezuela in den Jahren 1911-1913, unternommen und herausgegeben im Auftrage und mit Mitteln des Baessler-Instituts in Berlin von Theodor Koch-Gruenberg, Dritter Band: Ethnographie, Verlag Strecker und Schroeder in Stuttgart, 1923."@en ;
    skos:note "The catalogue line of the archive copy is wrong in three particulars and the title page was preferred, as SPEC section 10 requires. The catalogue gives the title as Vom Roraima, the publisher as Berlin, Dietrich Reimer (Ernest Vohsen), and the year as 1917; the file delivers Vom Roroima, Stuttgart, Strecker und Schroeder, 1923. This is the same failure the holding recorded at intellectualcult00rasm, where a catalogue line named a different volume than the file delivered."@en ;
    skos:note "Access: identifier bub_br_1918_01011130, full text over the regular route, 1,174,957 characters. The tail is the publisher's own back matter followed by a notice of the Brasiliana Digital collection of the University of Sao Paulo, so the file ends where the book ends and is not one of the truncations the access table warns about. The running heads over the part read run 197, 198, 199, 201, 202, 203, 204, 205, 206, 207, 208, 210, 211, 212, 213, 215, 216, 217, 218, 219, 220, 221 without going backwards, so the page numbers cited are the printed ones. The optical reading of the body text is good; the chapter headings and the table of contents are badly damaged, and the chapter was therefore located by its running heads and not by a heading."@en ;
    skos:note "What was read at the wording: pages 194 to 221, that is the end of the chapter on the cure, the whole of the chapter on the Zauberarzt including the account of his training, and the opening of the chapter on the spells. The volume runs to some four hundred pages and the rest is not opened."@en .

pmw:ZaubersprucheDerTaulipangIndianer a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Koch-Gruenberg, Zaubersprueche der Taulipang-Indianer"@en, "Koch-Gruenberg, Zaubersprueche der Taulipang-Indianer"@de ;
    skos:note "The primary publication of the spells, named by Koch-Gruenberg himself in the footnote at page 220: Zaubersprueche der Taulipang-Indianer, in Archiv fuer Anthropologie, Neue Folge, Band XIII, Heft 4, pages 371 ff., Braunschweig 1915. Declared as a work of the corpus and deliberately left unopened in this run, so that queries/unopened-works.rq reports it."@en ;
    skos:note "Why it is not the work to prefer. The eleven spells are printed in volume III of 1923 in three parts each - the myth that leads to the spell, the original text with interlinear German, and the instruction for applying it - and the article of 1915 is the shorter publication. Which of the two carries the complete set is not established here and is the first question for whoever opens it."@en .

#
# The tradition
#

pmt:Taulipang a pm:Tradition ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VomRoroimaZumOrinocoIII ;
    rdfs:label "Taulipang transmission"@en, "Taulipang-Ueberlieferung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the Taulipang, a Carib-speaking people of the Roroima region on the border of northern Brazil and Venezuela, in which a Zauberarzt is made over ten to twenty-five years by a graded course of emetics, fasting and tobacco, receives helping spirits that arrive as rock crystals, sends his shadow up a cord or a liana while his body stays on the ground, and may be permanently unmade by two named foods."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered at places read and not registered first, because the passages were opened in the same run in which the tradition was decided on. The three decisions SPEC section 14 requires before the first node were made and are the three notes that follow."@en ;
    skos:note "One: the contact route, and it is undecided rather than absent, on evidence in the work itself. Against a route: the corpus is Carib, the region is the Guiana shield, the account of the training comes from two named Taulipang and carries no Christian or European element anywhere in it, and the recording is of 1911 to 1913. For a route: the same volume places a Benedictine mission on the upper Surumu with a Taulipang chief living at it and the padres' horses and cattle in the story, and the recorder's own comparative apparatus is built from nineteenth-century missionaries in the neighbouring country - Brett in British Guiana, Quandt the Moravian in Surinam, Schomburgk, Barrere, Rochefort. So contact with Christian missions is documented for the period of fixation, while no route to any magical corpus in this holding is shown. Absent would be false and documented would overstate; undecided is the honest value and it is the same correction the Azande, Nahua and Maori entries received. Until the question stands as its own node this tradition may not be counted as a party to a convergence."@en ;
    skos:note "Two: recension and edition. Vom Roroima zum Orinoco, volume III, Stuttgart, Strecker und Schroeder, 1923, from the title page in the full text and not from the catalogue line, which is wrong. The account of the training is the recorder's German rendering of what Akuli told him orally, and the recorder says at the head of it that he gives it exactly as it was told to him; the original wording is not printed beside it. The spells are the one part of this corpus where the original is printed, and they were not read in this run."@en ;
    skos:note "Three: the number of citable places. Fourteen, from pages 194 to 221, all read at the wording in this run. The volume runs to some four hundred pages, and the chapters on the neighbouring Schiriana, Waika and Yekuana - the last with a Zauberarzt chapter of its own at pages 380 to 388 - are not opened. Grown in places and not in names, as traditions/daoist.ttl is the model for."@en ;
    skos:note "The lease that holds this work is prima-materia#838, and it was opened after the reading rather than before it: this run chose its work from the registration state and the witness position rather than from a standing lease. The harvest note stands at that issue."@en ;
    skos:note "Why this transmission and not another. It was chosen under the testability-driven entrance, which SPEC section 14 ranks first: it names two conditions under which a capacity is destroyed, each tied to a named substance and a named mechanism, and that is the material a pm:falsifiedBy can be formed from. The independence-driven entrance is the second reason and the weaker one, because the route is undecided."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Theodor Koch-Gruenberg, Vom Roroima zum Orinoco, Band III: Ethnographie (Stuttgart: Strecker und Schroeder, 1923); and the same author's Zaubersprueche der Taulipang-Indianer, Archiv fuer Anthropologie, Neue Folge XIII.4 (Braunschweig, 1915)" ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:VomRoroimaZumOrinocoIII, pmw:ZaubersprucheDerTaulipangIndianer ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteUndecided ;
    dcterms:source "Theodor Koch-Gruenberg, Vom Roroima zum Orinoco, Band III: Ethnographie (Stuttgart: Strecker und Schroeder, 1923), pp. 203-209" .

#
# The apprenticeship, and what it claims about its own length
#

pmp:TaulipangZauberarztApprenticeship a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VomRoroimaZumOrinocoIII ;
    rdfs:label "The making of a Zauberarzt: the graded course of emetics, fasting and tobacco"@en, "Der Werdegang des Zauberarztes: die gestufte Folge von Brechmitteln, Fasten und Tabak"@de ;
    skos:definition "The account Akuli gave of his own training, which the recorder says he reproduces exactly as it was told to him. Preparation begins between the tenth and twelfth year under an older Zauberarzt. The boys first drink water until they bring it up again, then rest five days. Then come emetics made from the finely ground bark of named trees stirred into water, each taken daily for a whole week in a fixed order: prle-yeg, mure-yeg, dzalaura-yeg, peune-yeg, mese-yeg, maualualekalu-yeg, the cinnamon tree maipaime, the wild cashew zoloi-yeg, then the liana kozokozo which is said to cut through all the entrails so that the novices bring up much blood, then the step-shaped liana kapeyenkuma(x)pe, then the bark makalaula, then elikaua, and last ayug. They vomit into a canoe and drink from it again and again. Powdered tube-shaped cicada nests are mixed into the emetics so that the boys get a beautiful voice. Finally they drink strong tobacco brew through the mouth and very strong tobacco brew through the nose, for one day only. The master also twists two cords from his wife's hair, puts them into the boys' nostrils and draws them slowly out through the mouth until blood comes."@en ;
    skos:note "The diet runs through the whole of it: only small birds, small fish and light kaschiri made from manioc cakes. The cakes are dried ten days in the sun; the master cuts a round hole in the middle, and the novice eats outward from the middle crumb by crumb so that the hole stays round, until it is about as wide as two hands can span. The recorder says he cannot explain this last practice and names two neighbouring usages of the same shape on other occasions, which is the right way round: the observation stands and the interpretation is marked as missing."@en ;
    skos:note "Two things in the procedure are instruments rather than ordeals, and the transmission says so. The cicada nests are for the voice, and the voice is what the office is carried out with. The hair cord is the ladder the shadow climbs; it is the same device that gives a youth at puberty and a hunter their power, by the same method of transfer, and it is named karauali."@en ;
    skos:note "A second and much shorter account of the same thing stands beside it from Katura: five nights of bark dissolved in water, a different mixture each night, vomiting each time, then tobacco brew, eating nothing and growing very thin, then gathering named leaves, binding them into the bundle the Zauberarzt later uses at a cure, climbing up with it, returning, and being a Zauberarzt. The recorder calls it very summary and says little can be done with it. Both are kept here in one node because they are two renderings of one procedure by two men of the same people, and the difference between ten years and five nights is a difference in the telling and not in the office."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Taulipang ;
    dcterms:source "Theodor Koch-Gruenberg, Vom Roroima zum Orinoco, Band III: Ethnographie (Stuttgart: Strecker und Schroeder, 1923), pp. 203-208, the accounts of Akuli and Katura" .

pmc:TaulipangLongerStudyStrongerShaman a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VomRoroimaZumOrinocoIII ;
    rdfs:label "The longer the study lasts, the stronger the Zauberarzt becomes"@en, "Je laenger das Studium waehrt, desto staerker wird der Zauberarzt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The claim the transmission makes about its own training, stated as a rule and not as encouragement. The apprenticeship lasts as a rule about ten years, sometimes sixteen to twenty, even twenty-five; and the longer the study lasts, the stronger the Zauberarzt becomes."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as its own node because of its form, which is rare in this holding. It is a monotonic claim relating a quantity somebody could measure - the years a man studied - to a magnitude the transmission treats as real, and it is not hedged by a threshold or a sufficiency clause. Almost every claim about attainment in this holding either names a term after which the thing is had or names no quantity at all; this one says more is more, without end."@en ;
    skos:note "What it would take to put it to a test is also what makes it safe. Strength here is not given any mark: nothing in the passage says what a stronger Zauberarzt does that a weaker one cannot, and the one measure of competence the corpus does supply - the number of Mauari that have fallen - is a criterion for release and not for strength. So the claim is comparative without a comparand, and the node records the absence rather than supplying one."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by Akuli, a named Taulipang, of his own training; recorded by Theodor Koch-Gruenberg in the Roroima region of northern Brazil between 1911 and 1913 and published by him in 1923. The language of the gathering was the recorder's German rendering of Akuli's oral account, which the recorder says at the head of the passage he gives exactly as it was told to him; the Taulipang wording is not carried beside it, and the technical terms are printed in Taulipang throughout." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Taulipang ;
    dcterms:source "Theodor Koch-Gruenberg, Vom Roroima zum Orinoco, Band III: Ethnographie (Stuttgart: Strecker und Schroeder, 1923), p. 205" .

pmc:TaulipangNeverOneNoviceAlone a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VomRoroimaZumOrinocoIII ;
    rdfs:label "Never one novice alone, and with four of them always two masters"@en, "Niemals ein Novize allein, und bei vier stets zwei Lehrmeister"@de ;
    skos:definition "A rule about the composition of the group rather than about the operation. A Zauberarzt is never alone: he always has a companion, whom he often brings up himself, and some have three or four. It is never one novice; at least two are taught together, sometimes three, sometimes four, and with four novices there are always two masters."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because the holding has little of this and the ratio is stated rather than implied. What it is for is not said. Two readings are open and the passage decides neither: that the office requires a second person in the work, which the cure does, since another Zauberarzt is the one who brings the novices' souls back at pmc:TaulipangSoulRetrievedByTheMaster; or that teaching two at once is simply how it is done. The first would make the pairing part of the capacity and the second part of the schooling."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by Akuli, a named Taulipang; recorded by Theodor Koch-Gruenberg in the Roroima region between 1911 and 1913, published 1923, in the recorder's German rendering of the oral account, with the Taulipang wording not carried beside it." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Taulipang ;
    dcterms:source "Theodor Koch-Gruenberg, Vom Roroima zum Orinoco, Band III: Ethnographie (Stuttgart: Strecker und Schroeder, 1923), p. 205" .

#
# The sign announced in advance, and the criterion of release
#

pmc:TaulipangMauariCrystalFallsIntoThePocket a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VomRoroimaZumOrinocoIII ;
    rdfs:label "Each night a Mauari comes as a rock crystal and falls into the novice's pocket"@en, "Jede Nacht kommt ein Mauari als Kristallstein und faellt in die Tasche des Novizen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The sign of attainment, announced to the novice before it happens. After five or six months of nightly singing and morning dancing, every night one Mauari comes for each novice, arriving as a rock crystal and falling into his pocket, a hunting bag of jaguar or otter skin or knitted cotton. The Mauari then passes out of the crystal into the boy's body. After a time a second comes, after a time a third, and so on, until each boy has a whole number of them."@en ;
    skos:note "This is a sign of success of the kind SPEC section 15 says must be counted separately: it is told to the novice in advance, so he reports it, and the base rate is unknown because nobody measures the same sign in anyone who was not told to expect it. It is entered and it is not counted among the conditions of failure."@en ;
    skos:note "Two things nevertheless make it stronger than most of its register. The crystals are real objects, found in several colours at Roroima and at the Serra das Cristaes, and the recorder says he repeatedly watched Zauberaerzte carry them about and blow tobacco smoke on them at night while murmuring formulas - so the thing that arrives is an object a bystander can see, even if its arrival is not. And the arrival is countable, which is what makes the criterion of release at pmc:TaulipangReleaseWhenManyHaveFallen possible at all."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by Akuli, a named Taulipang, of his own training; recorded by Theodor Koch-Gruenberg in the Roroima region between 1911 and 1913, published 1923, in the recorder's German rendering of the oral account. The recorder adds in his own voice, in square brackets, that he several times observed the crystals in use on the journey; that part is his own observation and not Akuli's testimony." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Taulipang ;
    dcterms:source "Theodor Koch-Gruenberg, Vom Roroima zum Orinoco, Band III: Ethnographie (Stuttgart: Strecker und Schroeder, 1923), p. 208" .

pmc:TaulipangReleaseWhenManyHaveFallen a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VomRoroimaZumOrinocoIII ;
    rdfs:label "The master may release the novices when many Mauari have fallen down"@en, "Der Zauberarzt darf die Novizen entlassen, wenn viele Mauari herabgefallen sind"@de ;
    skos:definition "The criterion that ends the apprenticeship, and the whole of it: then the Zauberarzt can release the novices, when many Mauari have fallen down. The training is over and the novices are finished Zauberaerzte who can now exercise the office."@en ;
    skos:note "A count without a number, and that is the finding. The apprenticeship is the most heavily quantified procedure in this part of the holding - a week per bark, five days of rest, ten days of drying, five or six months of singing, ten to twenty-five years in all - and the one quantity that decides whether a man is finished is left as many. Who judges that it is many is the master, and the thing judged is in the novice's body."@en ;
    skos:note "It is therefore not a condition of failure and must not be read as one. Nothing says what happens to a novice in whom few Mauari fall, nothing fixes a term after which the master gives up, and nothing names an outcome that would end the training without the office. The search at pmp:TaulipangDefectusSearch looked for such a term and did not find one."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by Akuli, a named Taulipang, of his own training; recorded by Theodor Koch-Gruenberg in the Roroima region between 1911 and 1913, published 1923, in the recorder's German rendering, with the decisive clause given in quotation marks as Akuli's own words." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Taulipang ;
    dcterms:source "Theodor Koch-Gruenberg, Vom Roroima zum Orinoco, Band III: Ethnographie (Stuttgart: Strecker und Schroeder, 1923), p. 208" .

# ANKER-ANHANG-HIER
